(228223, 13015, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC</b> Hey there andrewm.<br/>Fits The Challenge-Yes <br/>Composition-Nice but could of been better. <br/>Background-Fantastic sky shot, great colors. <br/>My Opinion-Shot is well done and yes, I''ve looked at many similar stock shots. This is nice. I do think this could of been a bit more crisp/sharp. The colors are very good. Keep up your good work.</td>', 'CC Hey there andrewm.Fits The Challenge-Yes Composition-Nice but could of been better. Background-Fantastic sky shot, great colors. My Opinion-Shot is well done and yes, I''ve looked at many similar stock shots. This is nice. I do think this could of been a bit more crisp/sharp. The colors are very good. Keep up your good work.', '2003-03-03 21:02:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(228287, 13000, 6100, '<td valign="top">Thanks to all who commented and voted. I learned something from each of the comments and I do appreciate them! I enjoyed making and sharing this image. My camera is in the shop for the next couple of weeks (for a through cleaning) but I''ll be back in the challenges as soon as possible.<br/> <br/>Bob</td>', 'Thanks to all who commented and voted. I learned something from each of the comments and I do appreciate them! I enjoyed making and sharing this image. My camera is in the shop for the next couple of weeks (for a through cleaning) but I''ll be back in the challenges as soon as possible. Bob', '2003-03-03 21:55:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(228497, 13082, 6325, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all the wonderful comments and the very good and on target critiques. <br/>For those who suggested flipping the picture to have the blue in the upper left - I tried this and it is a much better photograph! I''ll look at things from all angles next time.<br/>Another repeat input was the "not white stars". The reason they are dark is that the flag is strongly backlighted. The red and white stripes and blue field are translucent material and that gives the great lighting. The stars are doubled so that they show white from both sides and I think they are a different material - so when backlighted, they show up as a shadow and dark. Spot editing might have been a good choice here, but ... I''ll try something to see if I can improve this.<br/>Again, thanks for the inputs on this photograph and the the prior ones - the inputs really help.<br/>Dennis<br/></td>', 'Thanks for all the wonderful comments and the very good and on target critiques. For those who suggested flipping the picture to have the blue in the upper left - I tried this and it is a much better photograph! I''ll look at things from all angles next time.Another repeat input was the "not white stars". The reason they are dark is that the flag is strongly backlighted. The red and white stripes and blue field are translucent material and that gives the great lighting. The stars are doubled so that they show white from both sides and I think they are a different material - so when backlighted, they show up as a shadow and dark. Spot editing might have been a good choice here, but ... I''ll try something to see if I can improve this.Again, thanks for the inputs on this photograph and the the prior ones - the inputs really help.Dennis', '2003-03-04 00:00:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(228602, 13082, 1037, '<td valign="top">I could see why the stars were dark. To me, that didn''t detract from the photo at all... the backlighting was the most appealing aspect of this photo to me. I also like it being backwards!<br/> <br/>Congratulations on coming 3rd.<br/> <br/>By the way... I thought I would let you know that after I left the comment about thinking I could render this in a 3D program, I decided to set that as a challenge to myself. I shouldn''t say things like that and not be able to back them up (otherwise it''s like that saying "your mouth is writing cheques your ass can''t cash!"). So stay tuned, I''m going to try, and if I fail, I''ll take the comment back! :)<br/> <br/></td>', 'I could see why the stars were dark. To me, that didn''t detract from the photo at all... the backlighting was the most appealing aspect of this photo to me. I also like it being backwards! Congratulations on coming 3rd. By the way... I thought I would let you know that after I left the comment about thinking I could render this in a 3D program, I decided to set that as a challenge to myself. I shouldn''t say things like that and not be able to back them up (otherwise it''s like that saying "your mouth is writing cheques your ass can''t cash!"). So stay tuned, I''m going to try, and if I fail, I''ll take the comment back! :) ', '2003-03-04 03:11:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(228617, 13062, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By InSpZiL<br/> <br/>Composition - Yet another great picture Gordon.  Framing is very good.  I like the corner to corner approach.  I think if the heads and paddles were at the same angle, you might''ve won this week.  The contrast between background and subject is really good and the green of the water really makes the red on the boat stand out.  The water in itself is neat the way it accepts the sun on the top left and makes the frame sort of a gradient.  Doesn''t look like anywhere I''d want to swim though due to the odd color.<br/> <br/>Technical - Crisp and mostly clear, only a slight fuzz on the paddles probably due to them rowing which is sort of the whole point of why they''re out there so its an acceptable fuzz in my book.  The exposure is perfect.  The only thing I wonder about is if you cropped this pic that close to the back on purpose or if it was just taken this way.  I don''t see it being a huge problem, but if the back had a little more room I think it would only help.<br/> <br/>Overall - A great example of how to take something not so exciting and transforming into a nice image.  Very good capture.  I really like the contrasting colors and the corner to corner portrayal of the boat in the frame.  Very good shot! - Keep up the good work Gordon.<br/> <br/>-Bob</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy InSpZiL Composition - Yet another great picture Gordon.  Framing is very good.  I like the corner to corner approach.  I think if the heads and paddles were at the same angle, you might''ve won this week.  The contrast between background and subject is really good and the green of the water really makes the red on the boat stand out.  The water in itself is neat the way it accepts the sun on the top left and makes the frame sort of a gradient.  Doesn''t look like anywhere I''d want to swim though due to the odd color. Technical - Crisp and mostly clear, only a slight fuzz on the paddles probably due to them rowing which is sort of the whole point of why they''re out there so its an acceptable fuzz in my book.  The exposure is perfect.  The only thing I wonder about is if you cropped this pic that close to the back on purpose or if it was just taken this way.  I don''t see it being a huge problem, but if the back had a little more room I think it would only help. Overall - A great example of how to take something not so exciting and transforming into a nice image.  Very good capture.  I really like the contrasting colors and the corner to corner portrayal of the boat in the frame.  Very good shot! - Keep up the good work Gordon. -Bob', '2003-03-04 05:22:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(228620, 13120, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - This is a wonderful shot.  Very cute kid and you really capitalized on that for this shot.  He has a great profile!  I said a couple things in my comment during the challenge that pretty much still represent the way I feel about this.  The frame should''ve been filled a little bit more.  I think this would''ve help tone the lighting down a little.  And the lighting is a little overpowering.  I do think that it should be bright, overexposed to a lesser degree though.  Tremendous idea though and honestly one that is a couple very minor things away from being a ribbon photo.<br/> <br/>Technical - A little extra soft on the focus.  I don''t mind soft focus at all, when used appropriately.  This is one that I think it can be very effective on, just that this needs to be a little less soft.  Part of that might be the proximity of the subject to the camera.  The ultra bright light cannot be helping that either.  Exposure is obviously blown out purposely but I think a little too much here too.  I would''ve toned it down just bit, but definitely kept it pretty bright.<br/> <br/>Overall - I really liked this pic and still do.  You''ve done a great job capturing this little guys innocence and sense of wonder in a remarkable picture.  The eyes and eyelashes would sell this one in a heartbeat.  Even with all the criticism here, I think this is one of the ten best from this challenge.  Thanks for sharing it - Bob</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - This is a wonderful shot.  Very cute kid and you really capitalized on that for this shot.  He has a great profile!  I said a couple things in my comment during the challenge that pretty much still represent the way I feel about this.  The frame should''ve been filled a little bit more.  I think this would''ve help tone the lighting down a little.  And the lighting is a little overpowering.  I do think that it should be bright, overexposed to a lesser degree though.  Tremendous idea though and honestly one that is a couple very minor things away from being a ribbon photo. Technical - A little extra soft on the focus.  I don''t mind soft focus at all, when used appropriately.  This is one that I think it can be very effective on, just that this needs to be a little less soft.  Part of that might be the proximity of the subject to the camera.  The ultra bright light cannot be helping that either.  Exposure is obviously blown out purposely but I think a little too much here too.  I would''ve toned it down just bit, but definitely kept it pretty bright. Overall - I really liked this pic and still do.  You''ve done a great job capturing this little guys innocence and sense of wonder in a remarkable picture.  The eyes and eyelashes would sell this one in a heartbeat.  Even with all the criticism here, I think this is one of the ten best from this challenge.  Thanks for sharing it - Bob', '2003-03-04 05:36:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(228970, 13082, 3102, '<td valign="top">With all due respect to lisae, I really doubt that someone is going to go through all of the trouble of generating a 3D version of something like this.  I mean, that''s the whole point of stock photography -- if you don''t have the time to photograph something yourself, you go buy it!  So this was perfect in all respects.  Congrats on the ribbon!</td>', 'With all due respect to lisae, I really doubt that someone is going to go through all of the trouble of generating a 3D version of something like this.  I mean, that''s the whole point of stock photography -- if you don''t have the time to photograph something yourself, you go buy it!  So this was perfect in all respects.  Congrats on the ribbon!', '2003-03-04 14:40:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(229588, 13117, 1041, '<td valign="top"><b>Critique Club Critique</b> <br/> <br/><b>First Impression</b> <br/>I love sailing.  All those sailboats instantly remind me of Sweden, which is where I learned to sail.  Those were good times :)<br/> <br/><b>(1) Composition / Content</b> <br/>Your photo "flows" very nicely.  The pier on the left leads the eye into the photo, it then travels along the boats and ends up on the big boat on the right.  The dramatic sky and the little bird (perfectly timed to be surrounded by clouds) provide additional accents and places of interest.  <br/> <br/><b>(2) Background</b> <br/>The sky is very dramatic with the white clouds on the dark blue sky.  Did you use a polarizing filter for this?  It worked well.  The bottle green of the water is also nice, and the two frame the rows of mainly white boats very well.<br/> <br/><b>(3) Camera Work / Technical</b> <br/>Good.  The photo is in focus, the horizon level, and there''s a nice range of contrast and colors.  <br/> <br/><b>(4) Post-processing</b> <br/>Looks fine to me.  I like the simple white frame that you''ve chosen, it complements the picture well without being distracting.<br/> <br/><b>(5) Meeting the Challenge</b> <br/>Absolutely.  I could easily see this in a sailing magazine or brochure.<br/> <br/><b>(6) My Opinion</b> <br/>As I already mentioned, I like sailing.  I think you''ve captured the feeling of being out there really well, I can almost here the lines click against the masts.   A very nice photo, and I think your score reflects it.  I really have nothing to add to improve it.  :)<br/> <br/>Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review.<br/> <br/>Franziska.</td>', 'Critique Club Critique  First Impression I love sailing.  All those sailboats instantly remind me of Sweden, which is where I learned to sail.  Those were good times :) (1) Composition / Content Your photo "flows" very nicely.  The pier on the left leads the eye into the photo, it then travels along the boats and ends up on the big boat on the right.  The dramatic sky and the little bird (perfectly timed to be surrounded by clouds) provide additional accents and places of interest.   (2) Background The sky is very dramatic with the white clouds on the dark blue sky.  Did you use a polarizing filter for this?  It worked well.  The bottle green of the water is also nice, and the two frame the rows of mainly white boats very well. (3) Camera Work / Technical Good.  The photo is in focus, the horizon level, and there''s a nice range of contrast and colors.   (4) Post-processing Looks fine to me.  I like the simple white frame that you''ve chosen, it complements the picture well without being distracting. (5) Meeting the Challenge Absolutely.  I could easily see this in a sailing magazine or brochure. (6) My Opinion As I already mentioned, I like sailing.  I think you''ve captured the feeling of being out there really well, I can almost here the lines click against the masts.   A very nice photo, and I think your score reflects it.  I really have nothing to add to improve it.  :) Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review. Franziska.', '2003-03-05 09:06:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(229603, 13068, 4482, '<td valign="top">Greatings from the CC club!<br/> <br/>First off I truly enjoy this shot. <br/> <br/>THEME: Meets the challenge.<br/> <br/>FOCUS and LIGHTING: crisp focus and the lighting is ok. For stock I would''ve prefered a completely white background. It would make for a better sell as purity is of the essence in this category. The shadows behind the markers are somewhat distracting and take away from the purity of the photo. A second lightsource would solve this issue.<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION: I think the cropping is perfect! I love the angle of the lines and the fact that they are perfectly straight. I wouldn''t change a thing.<br/> <br/>The photo is simple as it should be... it does miss a bit of WOW factor but that''s the hardest part of photography IMO. I still have lots of problems with that!!!  :-)<br/> <br/>All in all, good job!<br/> <br/>YAnik</td>', 'Greatings from the CC club! First off I truly enjoy this shot.  THEME: Meets the challenge. FOCUS and LIGHTING: crisp focus and the lighting is ok. For stock I would''ve prefered a completely white background. It would make for a better sell as purity is of the essence in this category. The shadows behind the markers are somewhat distracting and take away from the purity of the photo. A second lightsource would solve this issue. COMPOSITION: I think the cropping is perfect! I love the angle of the lines and the fact that they are perfectly straight. I wouldn''t change a thing. The photo is simple as it should be... it does miss a bit of WOW factor but that''s the hardest part of photography IMO. I still have lots of problems with that!!!  :-) All in all, good job! YAnik', '2003-03-05 09:17:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(229639, 12872, 4380, '<td valign="top">CONGRATS!!!!! im amazed with the title....quite looooooong...</td>', 'CONGRATS!!!!! im amazed with the title....quite looooooong...', '2003-03-05 11:58:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(229668, 13021, 1024, '<td valign="top">Comment: <br/>Critique Club Critique <br/> <br/>(1) COMPOSITION (CONTENT) This is a very simple photograph. The absolute simplicity of the photograph is its real strength. The composition is probaby the best possible with such props as you have chosen to use.  The addition of the text is very important here, as the photograph without that would be too stark.<br/> <br/>(2) BACKGROUND I realy have nothing I can add about the lack of background.  The white sets off the paperclips well.<br/> <br/>(3) CAMERA WORK ,TECHNICAL The DOF is very good, all of the important parts are in sharp focus. <br/> <br/>(4) DIGITAL PROCESSING ,TECHNICAL Your post processing is good. Good use of black and white. I do feel that perhaps you have oversharpened a bit because the paperclips seem a bit "off".<br/> <br/>(5)MEETING THE CHALLENGE You have done a good job of meeting the challenge in an original way. <br/> <br/>(6) MY OPINION ON THE PHOTO The subject here is perhaps a bit oversimplified. The way you executed it is alright. There is not a whole lot here to hold my interest for very long.  I am glad to see that you did not spend much time on it.</td>', 'Comment: Critique Club Critique  (1) COMPOSITION (CONTENT) This is a very simple photograph. The absolute simplicity of the photograph is its real strength. The composition is probaby the best possible with such props as you have chosen to use.  The addition of the text is very important here, as the photograph without that would be too stark. (2) BACKGROUND I realy have nothing I can add about the lack of background.  The white sets off the paperclips well. (3) CAMERA WORK ,TECHNICAL The DOF is very good, all of the important parts are in sharp focus.  (4) DIGITAL PROCESSING ,TECHNICAL Your post processing is good. Good use of black and white. I do feel that perhaps you have oversharpened a bit because the paperclips seem a bit "off". (5)MEETING THE CHALLENGE You have done a good job of meeting the challenge in an original way.  (6) MY OPINION ON THE PHOTO The subject here is perhaps a bit oversimplified. The way you executed it is alright. There is not a whole lot here to hold my interest for very long.  I am glad to see that you did not spend much time on it.', '2003-03-05 12:35:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(229678, 13093, 1024, '<td valign="top">Comment: <br/>Critique Club Critique <br/> <br/>(1) COMPOSITION (CONTENT) This is a wonderful photograph.  I love the the compositional use of framing with the branch of the tree.  I also like the way the light is hitting the front of the old school house, it adds a depth that would not be there if you had chosen to shoot this at a different time of day.  Your lighting choice was superb!<br/> <br/>(2) BACKGROUND There are a few minor details in the background that would be distracting if they were in the foreground, but you have managed to keep them at a minimum and I am not bothered by them.  <br/> <br/>(3) CAMERA WORK ,TECHNICAL The DOF is very good, all of the important parts are in sharp focus. Exposure is right on.<br/> <br/>(4) DIGITAL PROCESSING ,TECHNICAL Your post processing is good. Wonderful use of black and white. Is very reminiscent of an old movie... perhaps "To Kill a Mockingbird". You have managed to keep your whites very crisp without losing your blacks. That is not always easy to do. <br/> <br/>(5)MEETING THE CHALLENGE You have done a fantastic job of meeting the challenge in a refreshing way. <br/> <br/>(6) MY OPINION ON THE PHOTO The subject here is wonderful. The way you executed it is great. This was one of the best of the stock pics. I am happy to see your score reflecting this. Well done!</td>', 'Comment: Critique Club Critique  (1) COMPOSITION (CONTENT) This is a wonderful photograph.  I love the the compositional use of framing with the branch of the tree.  I also like the way the light is hitting the front of the old school house, it adds a depth that would not be there if you had chosen to shoot this at a different time of day.  Your lighting choice was superb! (2) BACKGROUND There are a few minor details in the background that would be distracting if they were in the foreground, but you have managed to keep them at a minimum and I am not bothered by them.   (3) CAMERA WORK ,TECHNICAL The DOF is very good, all of the important parts are in sharp focus. Exposure is right on. (4) DIGITAL PROCESSING ,TECHNICAL Your post processing is good. Wonderful use of black and white. Is very reminiscent of an old movie... perhaps "To Kill a Mockingbird". You have managed to keep your whites very crisp without losing your blacks. That is not always easy to do.  (5)MEETING THE CHALLENGE You have done a fantastic job of meeting the challenge in a refreshing way.  (6) MY OPINION ON THE PHOTO The subject here is wonderful. The way you executed it is great. This was one of the best of the stock pics. I am happy to see your score reflecting this. Well done!', '2003-03-05 12:47:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(229712, 13098, 6172, '<td valign="top">Very nice photo with attractive colors and an interesting angle.  Very clean focus and really nice lighting except for the thin gray area in the upper right corner.  A small spot of glare on the middle (esp. upper) of the laptop.  Plenty of empty space for text.  I think the criticism for the angle and the (very small) shadow made by the laptop were unwarranted, the angle adds alot to the shot IMO.  I bet this shot was close to a ribbon?</td>', 'Very nice photo with attractive colors and an interesting angle.  Very clean focus and really nice lighting except for the thin gray area in the upper right corner.  A small spot of glare on the middle (esp. upper) of the laptop.  Plenty of empty space for text.  I think the criticism for the angle and the (very small) shadow made by the laptop were unwarranted, the angle adds alot to the shot IMO.  I bet this shot was close to a ribbon?', '2003-03-05 13:22:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(230220, 12806, 5212, '<td valign="top">Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Meeting the Challenge:<br/>This might be appropriate if someone was looking for a party theme photo, combination of colors and patterns, or maybe an Easter or Candy theme.  Not what I would consider a typical stock photo, but with today''s diversity it might well work for a particular need.<br/> <br/>Composition:<br/>The eggs do stand out to me, but the patterns in the bowl are a little distracting.  The brightness of the center helps create the focal point.  I am not personally wild about centered photos, and this is a little off from left to right.  I might have left the wider horizontal edge at the bottom to give it a little "anchor" rather than having it at the top.  I like the dark background, might have wanted to increase darkness just slightly more in levels to help even out the blackish areas in such.<br/> <br/>Technical Quality:  A bit over exposed in the middle, which detracts from the clarity in the middle section, esp with the little candies. I think you did well considering you were using a magnifying glass, though!<br/> <br/>Creativity:  Very good in this area, your photo was definitely unique.<br/> <br/>Overall conclusion: I think your idea was good, but the bowl in the photo may really haved caused problems with exposure, and made the photo a little too busy and a little questionable about the theme of this "stock" photo.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions, please feel free to email me.<br/> <br/>Kandice <br/> <br/></td>', 'Critique Club: Meeting the Challenge:This might be appropriate if someone was looking for a party theme photo, combination of colors and patterns, or maybe an Easter or Candy theme.  Not what I would consider a typical stock photo, but with today''s diversity it might well work for a particular need. Composition:The eggs do stand out to me, but the patterns in the bowl are a little distracting.  The brightness of the center helps create the focal point.  I am not personally wild about centered photos, and this is a little off from left to right.  I might have left the wider horizontal edge at the bottom to give it a little "anchor" rather than having it at the top.  I like the dark background, might have wanted to increase darkness just slightly more in levels to help even out the blackish areas in such. Technical Quality:  A bit over exposed in the middle, which detracts from the clarity in the middle section, esp with the little candies. I think you did well considering you were using a magnifying glass, though! Creativity:  Very good in this area, your photo was definitely unique. Overall conclusion: I think your idea was good, but the bowl in the photo may really haved caused problems with exposure, and made the photo a little too busy and a little questionable about the theme of this "stock" photo. If you have any questions, please feel free to email me. Kandice  ', '2003-03-06 01:00:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(230379, 12849, 4493, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comment:<br/> <br/>This is a very dramatic shot! You have captured a nice feel that could be used in an add for many things. Your gradients are very good. You have adjusted the contrast and brightness well to bring out the feel of the clouds. I wish that this tree was in a field by itself, the surround trees take away from some of the impact of the larger one. This is not a situation you can control, and you''ve done well to minimize the other trees overall impact and made the main focus the light area behind the large tree. Usually most would say that the main subject being at the center of the frame doesn''t work, but in this case it works well. The framing by the light cloud area behind it makes it fit. Your post processing work is good, no real artifacts or signs of oversharpening. <br/> <br/>I like what you''ve done here. It is a strong shot and for all the winter shots of trees we''ve seen lately, this is one of the top ones.</td>', 'Critique Club Comment: This is a very dramatic shot! You have captured a nice feel that could be used in an add for many things. Your gradients are very good. You have adjusted the contrast and brightness well to bring out the feel of the clouds. I wish that this tree was in a field by itself, the surround trees take away from some of the impact of the larger one. This is not a situation you can control, and you''ve done well to minimize the other trees overall impact and made the main focus the light area behind the large tree. Usually most would say that the main subject being at the center of the frame doesn''t work, but in this case it works well. The framing by the light cloud area behind it makes it fit. Your post processing work is good, no real artifacts or signs of oversharpening.  I like what you''ve done here. It is a strong shot and for all the winter shots of trees we''ve seen lately, this is one of the top ones.', '2003-03-06 10:11:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(230645, 13108, 1360, '<td valign="top">I think this would have come out a lot better, if the globe was originally a more "realistic" looking earth color.  I also think that the state/city names are very destracting.<br/> <br/>I do, however, really like the low angle of this shot.  I like the sharp focus on texas, and how it gradually fades out when looking at the other states.  The stark, black background really highlights the brightness of the globe.</td>', 'I think this would have come out a lot better, if the globe was originally a more "realistic" looking earth color.  I also think that the state/city names are very destracting. I do, however, really like the low angle of this shot.  I like the sharp focus on texas, and how it gradually fades out when looking at the other states.  The stark, black background really highlights the brightness of the globe.', '2003-03-06 16:45:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(231444, 13087, 7173, '<td valign="top">Pretty, buy s nikon and you''ll win.</td>', 'Pretty, buy s nikon and you''ll win.', '2003-03-07 19:19:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(231486, 13054, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club COmments by Grayce<br/>The foreground in the lower left corner frames the image, but is a little busy and takes up too much of the frame. I can''t really identify what they are.  <br/>Aside from that the structure stands out nicely against the snowy, frosty trees.<br/>You''ve captured some wonderful texture here.  The stones in the walls are good.  <br/>The coloring seems nice and realistic, and there is no evidence of digital noise.  <br/>Exposure is good, with nice contrast of light and shadow. I''m fascinated by the cold of the snow, while the water isn''t frozen.  <br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce<br/></td>', 'Critique Club COmments by GrayceThe foreground in the lower left corner frames the image, but is a little busy and takes up too much of the frame. I can''t really identify what they are.  Aside from that the structure stands out nicely against the snowy, frosty trees.You''ve captured some wonderful texture here.  The stones in the walls are good.  The coloring seems nice and realistic, and there is no evidence of digital noise.  Exposure is good, with nice contrast of light and shadow. I''m fascinated by the cold of the snow, while the water isn''t frozen.  Regards, Grayce', '2003-03-07 21:02:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(231556, 13123, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>I really like the silhouette on the left bottom.  It gives an anchored feeling to the shot adding to the stability, I think.  I think, since the upper rays weren''t as noticeable, I might have cropped them out.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>Wonderful use of the available lighting.  I have tried many times to "capture" rays like this, and though I have come close to getting them, I have done nothing this effective.  I like the warmth of the sky in the background, and again, the silhouette adds to the picture.  The sky seems a bit grainy or noisy, especially towards the top, so again, cropping that section may help.<br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>This is an awesome shot, very well composed and executed.  It has a peaceful/serene feeling to it and is very pleasant to look at.  I think you have met the challenge, and done it in a creative, effective way.<br/></td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONI really like the silhouette on the left bottom.  It gives an anchored feeling to the shot adding to the stability, I think.  I think, since the upper rays weren''t as noticeable, I might have cropped them out. TECHNIQUEWonderful use of the available lighting.  I have tried many times to "capture" rays like this, and though I have come close to getting them, I have done nothing this effective.  I like the warmth of the sky in the background, and again, the silhouette adds to the picture.  The sky seems a bit grainy or noisy, especially towards the top, so again, cropping that section may help. OVERALL EFFECTThis is an awesome shot, very well composed and executed.  It has a peaceful/serene feeling to it and is very pleasant to look at.  I think you have met the challenge, and done it in a creative, effective way.', '2003-03-08 00:00:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(231609, 12969, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - Good choice of subject for stock photography IMO.  I think the simple aspect of this photo makes it very suitable.  The subject is very centered in this photo and I think it has to be.  You might have left a little more room at the top and bottom of the photo though.  I don''t like the little blue dot on the left side of the apple.  It is pretty distracting and I can''t think of what would cause it.  This background might be a little light.  I''ll elaborate more in the technical section.<br/> <br/>Technical - Most of the problems that I see with this picture have to do with exposure.  The bottom half of this apple looks black.  It is really underexposed IMO.  I think the light needs to be brighter and moved perhaps a little more to the right judging by the highlight.  I think with the background being so light, it would be difficult to properly light and expose this photo so as not to wash the background out or leave the apple dark.  If the exposure was more the background would most likely was out.  This might be a good place to use the grocery bag background trick.  I think with that dark a background you could effectively light the subject and not wash the background out.  As it stands I feel the apple is underexposed.  The focus is good.  The crop is a little too tight.<br/> <br/>Overall - I like the simplicity of this photo and I think it meets the challenge.  The pic needs a little less contrast so that the exposure could be increased without fear of washing the background out.  The crop could leave a little more room on top and bottom.  Hope this has been helpful to you.  Good luck in future challenges Boyte - Bob</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - Good choice of subject for stock photography IMO.  I think the simple aspect of this photo makes it very suitable.  The subject is very centered in this photo and I think it has to be.  You might have left a little more room at the top and bottom of the photo though.  I don''t like the little blue dot on the left side of the apple.  It is pretty distracting and I can''t think of what would cause it.  This background might be a little light.  I''ll elaborate more in the technical section. Technical - Most of the problems that I see with this picture have to do with exposure.  The bottom half of this apple looks black.  It is really underexposed IMO.  I think the light needs to be brighter and moved perhaps a little more to the right judging by the highlight.  I think with the background being so light, it would be difficult to properly light and expose this photo so as not to wash the background out or leave the apple dark.  If the exposure was more the background would most likely was out.  This might be a good place to use the grocery bag background trick.  I think with that dark a background you could effectively light the subject and not wash the background out.  As it stands I feel the apple is underexposed.  The focus is good.  The crop is a little too tight. Overall - I like the simplicity of this photo and I think it meets the challenge.  The pic needs a little less contrast so that the exposure could be increased without fear of washing the background out.  The crop could leave a little more room on top and bottom.  Hope this has been helpful to you.  Good luck in future challenges Boyte - Bob', '2003-03-08 08:14:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(231789, 12869, 527, '<td valign="top">Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen<br/> <br/>The first thing that jumped out at me was the blue refractions that distract   from an otherwise good image. I am assumming this was caused by the spot filter which I know nothing about. I''m guessing that it wasn''t even needed especially if you are getting odd effects like that. I like that blue color I just think it could have been used more effectively.<br/> <br/>I like the composition including the cropping quite a bit. I would have liked to see the golf balls arranged a little more evenly amd symmetrical. It is a small thing but I think it is important especially for stock photography where things need to be pretty perfect. The lighting appears to be a little too low, physically. Raising it up would help give a little more definition to the background balls. I might have experimented with a secondary light source of another color such as blue set at a very low illuminocity to slightly fill in the shadow areas with some subtle color.<br/> <br/>The narrow DOF is pretty good, however I would have preferred seeing the entire front three balls in sharp focus. There still would have been plenty of effective blurring for the rest of the balls. If you meant to have the entire focus on the front middle ball than I think you should have position it even closer to the camera relative to the other balls. More of a V shape.<br/> <br/>The color is good as is the clarity and it meets the challenge very well. It is a pretty interesting image but it just needs a little more work in regards to lighting, DOF, and arrangement to make it look like true stock photo quality. Nice job.<br/> <br/>Tim</td>', 'Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen The first thing that jumped out at me was the blue refractions that distract   from an otherwise good image. I am assumming this was caused by the spot filter which I know nothing about. I''m guessing that it wasn''t even needed especially if you are getting odd effects like that. I like that blue color I just think it could have been used more effectively. I like the composition including the cropping quite a bit. I would have liked to see the golf balls arranged a little more evenly amd symmetrical. It is a small thing but I think it is important especially for stock photography where things need to be pretty perfect. The lighting appears to be a little too low, physically. Raising it up would help give a little more definition to the background balls. I might have experimented with a secondary light source of another color such as blue set at a very low illuminocity to slightly fill in the shadow areas with some subtle color. The narrow DOF is pretty good, however I would have preferred seeing the entire front three balls in sharp focus. There still would have been plenty of effective blurring for the rest of the balls. If you meant to have the entire focus on the front middle ball than I think you should have position it even closer to the camera relative to the other balls. More of a V shape. The color is good as is the clarity and it meets the challenge very well. It is a pretty interesting image but it just needs a little more work in regards to lighting, DOF, and arrangement to make it look like true stock photo quality. Nice job. Tim', '2003-03-08 18:25:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(231809, 12863, 527, '<td valign="top">Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen<br/> <br/>Did yu mean to say that you didn''t get the colors that you wanted? Because I really like the colors due to the underexposure. Exposure is just a general term anyway and in regards to this particular shot you have actually properly exposed it.<br/> <br/>What I am having a little trouble with is how symmetrical it is compositionally but not visually. In that I mean that the physical elements such as the tree and the sun are very balanced but in regards to the tonal values the sun has much more weight and therefore is too heavy on the right side. I think it would have been more pproperly balanced if you had framed it a little more to the right to allow more space for the sun and a little less for the tree. This would clearly make the sun the main focal point with the tree in a supporting roll.<br/> <br/>Like I said, the exposure seems very good to me. I like the way the tree mysteriously blends into the dark landscape not drawing too much attention away from the sun. I enjoy the real subtle highlights to some of the shapes on the land. They perk my interest and make me start to wonder exactly what they are and where this beautiful place is.<br/> <br/>It is a simple image and a very calming and pleasing one. It meets the challenge well and apppears to be well executed with vibrant natural colors and values with clean details. Nice job.<br/> <br/>Tim</td>', 'Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen Did yu mean to say that you didn''t get the colors that you wanted? Because I really like the colors due to the underexposure. Exposure is just a general term anyway and in regards to this particular shot you have actually properly exposed it. What I am having a little trouble with is how symmetrical it is compositionally but not visually. In that I mean that the physical elements such as the tree and the sun are very balanced but in regards to the tonal values the sun has much more weight and therefore is too heavy on the right side. I think it would have been more pproperly balanced if you had framed it a little more to the right to allow more space for the sun and a little less for the tree. This would clearly make the sun the main focal point with the tree in a supporting roll. Like I said, the exposure seems very good to me. I like the way the tree mysteriously blends into the dark landscape not drawing too much attention away from the sun. I enjoy the real subtle highlights to some of the shapes on the land. They perk my interest and make me start to wonder exactly what they are and where this beautiful place is. It is a simple image and a very calming and pleasing one. It meets the challenge well and apppears to be well executed with vibrant natural colors and values with clean details. Nice job. Tim', '2003-03-08 19:18:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(232169, 12915, 6536, '<td valign="top">This would make a good stock photo. I like the cropping &amp; coloring.  Nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'This would make a good stock photo. I like the cropping & coloring.  Nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 20:51:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(232173, 12914, 6536, '<td valign="top">I like this shot. It has great coloring &amp; cropping. Would make a great stock photo. Great shot.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'I like this shot. It has great coloring & cropping. Would make a great stock photo. Great shot.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:00:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(232174, 13059, 6536, '<td valign="top">Nice cropping &amp; colors.  It has a good theme. Nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Nice cropping & colors.  It has a good theme. Nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:02:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(232179, 13024, 4162, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/>Composition-content<br/>The angle of the shot fits the angle that she is looking, providing space from her eyes into the empty space. Also leaves room for text which is an added benefit for stock. I think that the composition is pretty interesting, I am sure that many people would think that you should have cropped off the pole, but I think composition wise it would have been pretty uninteresting without it.<br/>Technical<br/>Sharpening looks too strong here, resulting in jagged lines. Either that, or in the resizing.<br/>My opinion<br/>I like the composition and choice of B/W, interesting perspective. I think that pictures of pictures don''t usually score too highly. Probably would have done much better without the compression/sharpening issues. Intreresting shot!</td>', 'Critique ClubComposition-contentThe angle of the shot fits the angle that she is looking, providing space from her eyes into the empty space. Also leaves room for text which is an added benefit for stock. I think that the composition is pretty interesting, I am sure that many people would think that you should have cropped off the pole, but I think composition wise it would have been pretty uninteresting without it.TechnicalSharpening looks too strong here, resulting in jagged lines. Either that, or in the resizing.My opinionI like the composition and choice of B/W, interesting perspective. I think that pictures of pictures don''t usually score too highly. Probably would have done much better without the compression/sharpening issues. Intreresting shot!', '2003-03-09 21:05:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(232194, 12972, 6536, '<td valign="top">Would make a nice stock photo. Great detail on the eyes. Nice colors &amp; cropping. Good job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Would make a nice stock photo. Great detail on the eyes. Nice colors & cropping. Good job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:20:51.000000', NULL, 0),
