(232207, 12942, 6536, '<td valign="top">Good cropping. It has nice lines. This would make a good stock photo. Nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Good cropping. It has nice lines. This would make a good stock photo. Nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:33:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(232209, 12927, 4162, '<td valign="top">Composition-content<br/>Still laughing about the "brain stew" comment! well, I gather from the comments that most people didn''t like the goo in the middle, myself included. Otherwise this is an excellent shot. Lovely lighting and focus is spot on! The contrast between the yellow and black is strong and has impact. Without the slime, perhaps even scraping it off, or chosing a composition to exclude most of it.. I like the corner composition, so I would chose for getting rid of the goo but keeping the same composition. Everything else about the shot is perfect, so perhaps a reshoot would provide a very sellable stock picture. Good Luck in the next challenge</td>', 'Composition-contentStill laughing about the "brain stew" comment! well, I gather from the comments that most people didn''t like the goo in the middle, myself included. Otherwise this is an excellent shot. Lovely lighting and focus is spot on! The contrast between the yellow and black is strong and has impact. Without the slime, perhaps even scraping it off, or chosing a composition to exclude most of it.. I like the corner composition, so I would chose for getting rid of the goo but keeping the same composition. Everything else about the shot is perfect, so perhaps a reshoot would provide a very sellable stock picture. Good Luck in the next challenge', '2003-03-09 21:36:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(232213, 13025, 6536, '<td valign="top">Good theme. Nice cropping. You might want to try this in black &amp; white. The white spots are a little distracting. Overall nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Good theme. Nice cropping. You might want to try this in black & white. The white spots are a little distracting. Overall nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:37:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(232217, 13090, 6536, '<td valign="top">This would make a good stock photo. I like the motion blur. It has nice colors &amp; cropping. Good job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'This would make a good stock photo. I like the motion blur. It has nice colors & cropping. Good job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:39:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(232224, 13010, 6536, '<td valign="top">Good angle. I like the colors &amp; cropping. Good stock photo. Nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'Good angle. I like the colors & cropping. Good stock photo. Nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:42:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(232229, 12888, 6536, '<td valign="top">I could see this in a stock photo book. Great lighting &amp; contrast. You might want to clone out the text on the light bulb. Great job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'I could see this in a stock photo book. Great lighting & contrast. You might want to clone out the text on the light bulb. Great job.Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2003-03-09 21:45:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(232289, 12940, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Interesting composition, meets the challenge, lens flare hurts it.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/> <br/>Great colors and the tree is awesome.  I like the sunset as well but the lens flare in the middle is a dead give-away.  Just like you knew it would...but what is most important is that you liked and appreciated the shot.<br/> <br/><b>Background</b> <br/>I like the way the rays disappear into the growing gloom at the top of the shot.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>Focus seems pretty good and contrast is excellent for the difference in light and shadow you have here.  If you could have somehow avoided the flair you would have a killer shot.<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical </b> <br/>Seems pretty good, you don''t have much grain even with the low light. Sharpness and contrast are good. <br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge </b> <br/>Fits the challenge well, I like that you left room for text in the upper left.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I don''t want to mention it again! I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a little to finish even higher than it did. Good job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  Interesting composition, meets the challenge, lens flare hurts it. Composition/ Content   Great colors and the tree is awesome.  I like the sunset as well but the lens flare in the middle is a dead give-away.  Just like you knew it would...but what is most important is that you liked and appreciated the shot. Background I like the way the rays disappear into the growing gloom at the top of the shot. Camera Work - Technical Focus seems pretty good and contrast is excellent for the difference in light and shadow you have here.  If you could have somehow avoided the flair you would have a killer shot. Digital Processing - Technical  Seems pretty good, you don''t have much grain even with the low light. Sharpness and contrast are good.  Fits The Challenge  Fits the challenge well, I like that you left room for text in the upper left. My Opinion On The Photo  I don''t want to mention it again! I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a little to finish even higher than it did. Good job, and keep up the good work.   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz ', '2003-03-09 22:39:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(232304, 12985, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comments by Grayce<br/>The coloring here is brilliant.  That sky is just perfect.  I love the lighting on the tree branches.  It shows some texture. As for your cropping, it would have been better to see the tips of the branches. The composition is too tight to accomodate any text that a stock agency would look for.<br/>Overall a pretty picture but lacking "WOW" factor.<br/>Good luck on future challenges.<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique Club Comments by GrayceThe coloring here is brilliant.  That sky is just perfect.  I love the lighting on the tree branches.  It shows some texture. As for your cropping, it would have been better to see the tips of the branches. The composition is too tight to accomodate any text that a stock agency would look for.Overall a pretty picture but lacking "WOW" factor.Good luck on future challenges.Regards, Grayce', '2003-03-09 23:06:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(232305, 12934, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comments by Grayce<br/>You did a wonderful job of isolating the subjects from the background.  The bg, being blackened out, is just right. <br/>The exposure is very good on the faces, and bodies, and the lighting was well used.<br/>The composition is good, and you''ve left room for potential text.<br/>Another good thing about this is that it shows action.<br/>Their faces show good expression too.<br/>Overall this is well done, and I can see it being used by a stock agency for lots of purposes.<br/>Good job!<br/>Best of luck on future challenges.<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique Club Comments by GrayceYou did a wonderful job of isolating the subjects from the background.  The bg, being blackened out, is just right. The exposure is very good on the faces, and bodies, and the lighting was well used.The composition is good, and you''ve left room for potential text.Another good thing about this is that it shows action.Their faces show good expression too.Overall this is well done, and I can see it being used by a stock agency for lots of purposes.Good job!Best of luck on future challenges.Regards, Grayce', '2003-03-09 23:13:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(236016, 12835, 1005, '<td valign="top">I really thought this might place higher!  Very nice shot, Justine.  I really like your props and set up.  I love the contradictions in the reel issue.  (sorry to laugh, but it just sounds sooo familiar!)</td>', 'I really thought this might place higher!  Very nice shot, Justine.  I really like your props and set up.  I love the contradictions in the reel issue.  (sorry to laugh, but it just sounds sooo familiar!)', '2003-03-12 12:59:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(237716, 13101, 2764, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club - <br/> <br/>I bet you thought I had forgotten all about your poor picture being held hostage for week.  Well, I didn''t.<br/> <br/>I voted on this photo, the critique club computer assigned it to me, I read all the comments, thought about my response a bit, was wondering why the background was so unblack when I remembered that you said you shot it on your mirror.  Why on a mirror?  I thought.  I am embarrassed to say that it was only THEN that I noticed this was not an unusually fat rose but actually a rose and it''s reflection.  I should have been clued in by the title but I wasn''t.  So perhaps others also missed the refection?<br/> <br/>This is really a lovely rose and I think if it were fixed up it could be sale quality.  I do wish that the reflection was clearer.  Perhaps the reflected image could be paler?  You could fix it up now that the challenge is over.  Perhaps just darken the shadows btween the two images so there is a sense of separation?  Do you find that feathery reflection at the very back edge of rose #2 distracting?  I don''t mind it but if you are cleaning the image up for possible sale of prints maybe that could be fixed too.  Also I think you need just a bit more room for the rose, it feels crowded to me and the thin orange border only enhances the feeling of confinement.  to me a rose should be free.  <br/> <br/>I think the image as a whole is too square.  If you backed up just a little you could put in more negative black space which would bring up the shape of the flower and maybe the perfect circle center of the reflection (my favorite part) would end up in that magic rule of thirds spot that everyne find so pleasing.  Is that a duplicate stem at the lower left?  Could you edit that out too?  it obscures that nice curled leaf.  Picky aren''t I.  <br/> <br/>I love the color - I think the seredipitous white balance error worked in your favor.  I LOVE to soft focus.  there have been many discussion of soft vs OFF focus.  This is a good example of soft.  There isn''t one sharp spot and other fussy ones. Instead it is all that the same focus, good use of your DOF.  The water droplets are just right and well placed.  And I love the texture, the smooth warm rose and the cool round drops of dew and the spikey fuzzy stem all make great contrasts.  The lighting is perfect, nice and even but still creating petal shadows.<br/> <br/>Your composition is too "in your face", meaning that it s too centered, too big for it''s frame.  there really isn''t any room for the meandering eye of the viewer.  the stem is a bit of a leading line but the flower is a giant blob.  Could it be placed more off center?  More background?  more shadows in the petals?  a center of focus?  I don''t see many of the classic design elements.  the rose is to big to be a shape, too uniform to have a pattern, nothing to balance it.  Maybe I''m wrong, maybe it need to be closer, rather than further away.  What would happen if you cropped it in and let the petals be the focus, would that play up that circular center tat I like?<br/> <br/>As far a stock photography, I agree that this probably would not sell.  Stock should be lovely but maybe less dominant.  A stock photo would be used to catch the eye but i must be able to share or be secondary to the message, be it an advertizement or a greeting card.  If this was on a white background sort of fading away it would make a nice card.  But I prefer this rose to stand out on it''s own as a framed print (oh, it could be a calendar page).<br/> <br/>So overall  I almost love this image, but not quite.  It almost catches my eye, but loses me.  It is nice enough to deserve some more work, not be abandoned.<br/> <br/>And the disclaimer: please remember that this is only my opinion and I am NOT an expert.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-03-25 11:22:20.</i></td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club -  I bet you thought I had forgotten all about your poor picture being held hostage for week.  Well, I didn''t. I voted on this photo, the critique club computer assigned it to me, I read all the comments, thought about my response a bit, was wondering why the background was so unblack when I remembered that you said you shot it on your mirror.  Why on a mirror?  I thought.  I am embarrassed to say that it was only THEN that I noticed this was not an unusually fat rose but actually a rose and it''s reflection.  I should have been clued in by the title but I wasn''t.  So perhaps others also missed the refection? This is really a lovely rose and I think if it were fixed up it could be sale quality.  I do wish that the reflection was clearer.  Perhaps the reflected image could be paler?  You could fix it up now that the challenge is over.  Perhaps just darken the shadows btween the two images so there is a sense of separation?  Do you find that feathery reflection at the very back edge of rose #2 distracting?  I don''t mind it but if you are cleaning the image up for possible sale of prints maybe that could be fixed too.  Also I think you need just a bit more room for the rose, it feels crowded to me and the thin orange border only enhances the feeling of confinement.  to me a rose should be free.   I think the image as a whole is too square.  If you backed up just a little you could put in more negative black space which would bring up the shape of the flower and maybe the perfect circle center of the reflection (my favorite part) would end up in that magic rule of thirds spot that everyne find so pleasing.  Is that a duplicate stem at the lower left?  Could you edit that out too?  it obscures that nice curled leaf.  Picky aren''t I.   I love the color - I think the seredipitous white balance error worked in your favor.  I LOVE to soft focus.  there have been many discussion of soft vs OFF focus.  This is a good example of soft.  There isn''t one sharp spot and other fussy ones. Instead it is all that the same focus, good use of your DOF.  The water droplets are just right and well placed.  And I love the texture, the smooth warm rose and the cool round drops of dew and the spikey fuzzy stem all make great contrasts.  The lighting is perfect, nice and even but still creating petal shadows. Your composition is too "in your face", meaning that it s too centered, too big for it''s frame.  there really isn''t any room for the meandering eye of the viewer.  the stem is a bit of a leading line but the flower is a giant blob.  Could it be placed more off center?  More background?  more shadows in the petals?  a center of focus?  I don''t see many of the classic design elements.  the rose is to big to be a shape, too uniform to have a pattern, nothing to balance it.  Maybe I''m wrong, maybe it need to be closer, rather than further away.  What would happen if you cropped it in and let the petals be the focus, would that play up that circular center tat I like? As far a stock photography, I agree that this probably would not sell.  Stock should be lovely but maybe less dominant.  A stock photo would be used to catch the eye but i must be able to share or be secondary to the message, be it an advertizement or a greeting card.  If this was on a white background sort of fading away it would make a nice card.  But I prefer this rose to stand out on it''s own as a framed print (oh, it could be a calendar page). So overall  I almost love this image, but not quite.  It almost catches my eye, but loses me.  It is nice enough to deserve some more work, not be abandoned. And the disclaimer: please remember that this is only my opinion and I am NOT an expert.', '2003-03-15 15:09:53.000000', '2003-03-25 11:22:20.000000', 0),
